WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.735
API serving introduces concurrency.

00:00:02.735 --> 00:00:04.770
Instead of one user waiting on one response,

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:08.415
the system may receive many requests with different prompt lengths, generation limits,

00:00:08.415 --> 00:00:09.480
and urgency.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.040
Batch tuning becomes a scheduling problem as much as a raw performance problem.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.790
The service has to decide how many requests to accept, how much work to group together,

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:18.838
and when to apply backpressure.

00:00:18.838 --> 00:00:21.970
Continuous batching and dynamic batching are common serving ideas.

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:22.715
At a high level,

00:00:22.715 --> 00:00:26.760
they let the runtime group active requests in ways that improve hardware utilization while

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.930
requests arrive and finish at different times.

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:32.480
This can improve throughput, but it does not remove latency tradeoffs.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:35.405
Users still care about queue delay, first-token latency, and timeouts.

00:00:35.405 --> 00:00:37.610
Concurrency limits, maximum prompt size,

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:41.820
and maximum generated tokens are part of batch tuning because they shape the work entering

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:42.520
the system.

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:46.560
Without limits, a few very large prompts can consume memory and delay everyone else.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:47.234
Fairness matters.

00:00:47.234 --> 00:00:51.235
A service should avoid letting one request pattern dominate the queue or make normal

00:00:51.235 --> 00:00:53.130
interactive requests feel unreliable.

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:55.305
Operational controls complete the picture.

00:00:55.305 --> 00:00:58.063
Rate limiting protects the service from demand spikes.

00:00:58.063 --> 00:01:02.410
Backpressure tells clients to slow down instead of silently building an unhealthy queue.

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:06.385
Monitoring should show queue depth, active sequences, token rates, errors,

00:01:06.385 --> 00:01:07.280
and resource use.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:09.600
The best throughput configuration is not just fast;

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.470
it is understandable and recoverable when demand changes.

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:15.735
For API platforms, define the service promise before tuning.

00:01:15.735 --> 00:01:18.755
Decide whether the priority is low latency for interactive users,

00:01:18.755 --> 00:01:21.970
high throughput for batch jobs, or a controlled balance between both.

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:25.900
Those goals lead to different queue limits, timeout values, and batching choices.

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:29.440
Without that service promise, a faster benchmark can still produce a worse product.
